<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
<p class="font-bold">Out of 10,000 people, 981 test positive.</p>
<div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-base">
<div class="border-2 border-ink p-3"><p class="font-bold">100 have the condition</p><p>90 correctly test positive.</p></div>
<div class="border-2 border-ink p-3"><p class="font-bold">9,900 do not</p><p>891 wrongly test positive.</p></div>
</div>
<svg viewBox="0 0 480 130" class="w-full" role="img" aria-labelledby="base-rate-title base-rate-desc">
<title id="base-rate-title">Of the positive test results, just 90 out of 981 are real</title>
<desc id="base-rate-desc">The small portion for 90 true positives is followed by a much larger portion for 891 false positives. True positives are about 9.2 percent of all positive tests.</desc>
<rect x="10" y="32" width="460" height="36" fill="#bd4b37" fill-opacity=".7"/>
<rect x="10" y="32" width="42.2" height="36" fill="#387a91"/>
<g fill="currentColor" font-size="18"><text x="10" y="22">All positive tests (981)</text><text x="10" y="97">90 real</text><text x="310" y="97">891 false alarms</text><text x="10" y="119" font-weight="bold">Only about 9.2% of positive tests indicate the condition</text></g>
</svg>
<figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">The test catches 90% of people with the condition and wrongly flags 9% of those without it. Because the condition is rare, false alarms greatly outnumber true alarms. The figures come from the entry’s invented example.</figcaption></figure>